      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"practical preservation\" argument is a classic case of using humanitarian concerns as a smokescreen to maintain colonial control over cultural heritage. While the risk of destruction in war zones is real, it is a failure of global security and diplomatic pressure, not a justification for museums to hoard stolen goods. The moral imperative to return artifacts cannot be suspended based on hypothetical risks; instead, the international community must work to protect source nations from conflict so these items can be safely returned.",
      "source": "target",
